---
title: What it takes to be an entrepreneur
description: >-
  Anger works. Authorship, daring, and trust work differently. A draft of how I
  think about the fuel.
pubDate: 2026-08-10
draft: true
---
There are at least two ways into entrepreneurship. One is simple. The other is slower but for me, the only one that makes sense.

## The anger path

You can be obsessed with proving something to someone.

Your fuel is anger. You do not need a careful “I.” You burn, you move, you collect the prizes. I have done this. It works. It ships. It also empties you, and when the target of the proof fades, so does the engine.

If that is your season, fine. Just know what you are running on.

## The other way

If you want a different fuel, these are the three things I keep returning to.

### 1. Take authorship of your life

Life will test you. Constantly. Hard days, closed doors, wrong markets, wrong timing.

Complaining about inflation, the state, gender inequality  as the story that explains why you cannot move — is a dead end. Those forces are real. They are not a substitute for authorship. You either hold the pen, or you hand it to the weather.

### 2. Dare to ask. Dare to dream.

Ask for the meeting. Ask for the grant. Ask for the introduction you think you have not earned yet. Dream past the version of yourself that only negotiates with what already feels safe.

Most of the ceiling is not market. It is the questions you never say out loud.

### 3. Resilience — which is trust

Resilience is not gritted teeth. At bottom it is trust in what is happening: the setback, the pivot, the quiet month, the almost.

Not blind optimism. Trust that this chapter belongs in the story you are writing  and that you stay in the room with it.

